CCCI probe host checks
======================

Run from the kernel root:

  make -C tools/testing/ccci-probe CC=clang test

The C tests compile the same buffer parser and one-shot gate used by
ccci_probe.ko, with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer by
default. All inputs are synthetic host RAM. The test suite neither loads a
module nor contacts a device. The Kconfig test uses the actual probe
Kconfig with isolated prerequisite symbols and temporary output files.
It does not regenerate the kernel's .config.

These tests validate software bounds and gate decisions, not whether the
phone's reserved memory is safe to read. They do not exercise the real
kernel module loader, workqueue, DT/stash retrieval, or hardware mappings.

The module accepts the known v2/v3 tag ABI. Unknown versions require a
separate review. Tag traversal follows LK's declared count; the last link
may be zero or a bounded forward end. A successful diagnostic requires a
complete chain and a usable smem_layout. Unknown tags are skipped only
after checking their entire declared payload. No decoded address is
followed, no reserved memory is written or released, and the built-in
ccci_util state is not populated.

At runtime, module loading validates only the ordinary-memory DT/stash
header. A missing or invalid header fails the load without mapping tags.
Load-time attempts to arm ccci_util_probe are rejected. After successful
loading, the root-only ccci_util_probe parameter can request one read.
The read-only status parameter distinguishes ready, queued, running,
done and failed, with an errno. Writing 0 neither cancels nor rearms.
The one-shot limit includes failed attempts and resets only on reload.

Actual reads remain capable of hanging or resetting the whole device.
Module unloading and timeouts are not hardware-fault recovery guarantees.
Deployment and hardware reads require a separately confirmed recovery plan.
